# notebook

## Chapter 1

you need to start small and work your way up to advance areas, **metaprograming** is about manupulating and inspecting the ast

```elixir
quote do: div(10,2)
```

macros recieves ast and returns ast (abstrac syntax tree)

```elixir
quote do: 1*3+3
```

```elixir
defmodule Math do
  defmacro say({:+, _, [lhs, rhs]}) do
    quote do
      lhs = unquote(lhs)
      rhs = unquote(rhs)
      result = lhs + rhs
      IO.puts "#{lhs} plus #{rhs} == #{result}"
      # result
    end 
  end

  defmacro say({:*, _, [lhs, rhs]}) do
    quote do
      lhs = unquote(lhs)
      rhs = unquote(rhs)
      result = lhs * rhs
      IO.puts "#{lhs} times #{rhs} == #{result}"
      result
    end
  end


  # this will puts "test" tree times since it is being baked into the ast
  defmacro test do
    quote do
      IO.puts("test")
      3
    end
  end

  ## even if it is a macro it will only prints one test
  defmacro unquoted_test do
      IO.puts("test")
      3
  end
end
```

```elixir
require Math

# for _ <- 1..3, do: Math.test
# for _ <- 1..3, do: Math.unquoted_test

```

You can be afraid of being a little unresponsible when learning a new skill .
"You can’t
be afraid to be a little irresponsible while you’re learning" awesome teaching in general.

macros must be defined withing a module

```elixir
defmodule ControlFlow do
  defmacro if!(expression, do: block) do
    quote do
      if not unquote(expression), do: unquote(block)
    end
  end
end
```

```elixir
require ControlFlow
ControlFlow.if! 2 == 5, do: "entre"
```

we can think of 'quote' and 'unquote' as the string interpolation of code. quote generates like `" "` and unquote injects like `#{}`

```elixir
number = 5
ast = quote do
  number * 10
end
# it fail cause it treats number as a variable not as a value assigned
Code.eval_quoted(ast)
```

```elixir
number = 5
ast = quote do
  unquote(number) * 10
end

# Code.eval_quoted(ast)
```

```elixir
ast = quote do
  ControlFlow.if! 2 == 5, do: "entre"
end
```

```elixir
first_expansion = Macro.expand_once(ast, __ENV__)
```

```elixir
second_expansion = Macro.expand_once(first_expansion, __ENV__)
```

the place where the code is injected is called context
